<worksheet>
    <title>
        Second order homogeneous differential equations with constant
        coefficients
    </title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Understand the general form of second order homogeneous
                    differential equations with constant coefficients.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Learn how to solve second order homogeneous differential
                    equations with constant coefficients using the
                    characteristic equation method.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Recognize the different types of solutions based on the
                    roots of the characteristic equation (real and distinct,
                    real and repeated, complex conjugate).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this worksheet we will be looking exclusively at linear second
            order differential equations with constant coefficients
            <men xml:id="eqgeneral">
                ay^{\prime\prime}+by^{\prime}+cy=g(t).
            </men>
        </p>

        <p>
            Notice that this also means that <m>a\ne 0</m>.
            We have encountered <xref ref="eqgeneral"/> in two contexts:
        </p>


<ul>
<li>
    <p>The spring mass system, <m> m\frac{d^{2}y}{dt^{2}}+b\frac{dy}{dt}+ky=0</m>,
        where <m>y</m> denotes displacement, and where <m>m</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>k</m>
        denote the mass, damping parameter, and spring constant, respectively.  
        
    </p>
</li>
<li>
    <p>
        The RLC circuit, <m>L\frac{d^{2} Q}{dt^{2}}+R\frac{d Q}{dt}+\frac{1}{C}Q =0</m>,
                where <m>Q</m> denotes charge, and where <m>L</m>, <m>R</m>, and <m>C</m> denote
                the inductance, resistance, and capacitance, respectively.
                (Here we assume there is no external voltage so that the equation is
                homogenous.)
    </p>
</li>

    </ul>
        <p>
            Initially we will make our life easier by looking at differential
            equations with <m>g(t)=0</m>.
            When <m>g(t)=0</m> we call the differential equation
            <em>homogeneous</em> and when <m>g(t)\ne 0</m> we call the
            differential equation <em>nonhomogeneous</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
            As discussed before this model shows up in the harmonic oscillator.
            We guess that the solution <m>y(t)</m> must be of an exponential
            form, i.e.
            <m>y(t)=e^{rt}</m>, and our job is to find the values of <m>r</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Substituting in the original equation we get that
            <md>
                e^{rt}(ar^{2}+br+c)=0
            </md>
            which implies that <m>r</m> is a root for the quadratic equation
            <md>
                ar^{2}+br+c=0.
            </md>
            Thus the solution depends on the values of <m>r</m> obtained by the
            quadratic equation
            <md>
                r=\frac{-b\pm \sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a}
            </md>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <section>
        <title>Distinct roots</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                When the characteristic equation has two distinct real roots
                <m>r_1</m> and <m>r_2</m>, the general solution of the
                differential equation is
                <md>
                    y(t)=c_1 e^{r_1 t}+c_2 e^{r_2 t}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <example>
            <p>
                Find the solution for
                <md>
                    y^{\prime\prime}+y^{\prime}-6y=0.
                </md>
                with <m>y(0)=0</m> and <m>y^{\prime}(0)=1</m>.
            </p>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The characteristic equation is
                    <md>
                        r^{2}+r-6=0,
                    </md>
                    which has roots <m>r_1=-3</m> and <m>r_2=2</m>.
                    Therefore, the general solution is
                    <md>
                        y(t)=c_1 e^{-3t}+c_2 e^{2t}.
                    </md>
                    Using the initial conditions <m>y(0)=0</m> and
                    <m>y^{\prime}(0)=1</m>, we get
                    <md>
                        c_1+c_2=0,\\
                    -3c_1+2c_2=1.
                    </md>
                    Solving this system gives <m>c_1=-\frac{1}{5}</m> and
                    <m>c_2=\frac{1}{5}</m>.
                    Hence, the solution is
                    <md>
                        y(t)=-\frac{1}{5} e^{-3t}+\frac{1}{5} e^{2t}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </section>

    <section>
        <title>Repeated roots</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                When the characteristic equation has a repeated real root
                <m>r</m>, the general solution of the differential equation is
                <md>
                    y(t)=c_1 e^{r t}+c_2 t e^{r t}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise workspace="1.0in">
            <p>
                Find the solution for
                <md>
                    y^{\prime\prime}-6y^{\prime}+9y=0.
                </md>
                with <m>y(0)=0</m> and <m>y^{\prime}(0)=1</m>.
            </p>
        </exercise>
    </section>

    <section>
        <title>Complex roots</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                When the characteristic equation has complex conjugate roots
                <m>r=\alpha \pm \beta i</m>, the general solution of the
                differential equation is
                <md>
                    y(t)=e^{\alpha t}(c_1 \cos(\beta t)+c_2 \sin(\beta t)).
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise workspace="1.0in">
            <p>
                Find the solution for
                <md>
                    y^{\prime\prime}+4y^{\prime}+13y=0.
                </md>
                with <m>y(0)=0</m> and <m>y^{\prime}(0)=1</m>.
            </p>
        </exercise>
    </section>
</worksheet>